From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: follow bridge FDB moves for
 port-addressed flows

A flow towards an rtl8_4 user port carries the port in its emulated head
tag, so hardware keeps delivering to the port the flow was resolved for
after the client has moved to another one. The software flowtable has
the same blind spot (its direct transmit path is bound to the port it
resolved at creation) and nothing lets a driver tear a flow down: the
cookie is opaque and nf_flow_table_cleanup() is per interface and lives
in a module this driver does not depend on. What the driver can do is
follow the move.

Register a switchdev notifier. The bridge learns the client's new port
from the first frame that reaches the CPU (ARP, DHCP, a new
connection's SYN) and reports it as SWITCHDEV_FDB_ADD_TO_DEVICE on the
new port device. Only a move within the flow's own VLAN-unaware
bridge, learned without a VLAN, counts: that is the one domain in
which the bridge itself would send the client's traffic to the new
port, whereas another bridge on the same switch learning the address
(a guest port claiming a trusted client's MAC) must not pull the flow
across, which the emulated head tag would otherwise do with no FDB or
VLAN check in the switch. Within the bridge the follower trusts
learning exactly as far as the bridge does. The atomic notifier looks
the address up in a hash of the fixed-port flows, queues nothing for
the addresses the bridge learns all day, for another bridge's learns,
for locked (MAB) entries or while a move for the address is already
waiting, and caps the items in flight; the work also requires the new
port to be forwarding. For every matching flow not already on that
port, the work item takes a nexthop with the new
port mask, deletes the flow entry and writes it back at the same index
(the key is unchanged, so the slot hardware hashed it to is still the
right one) with the new nexthop, and drops the old nexthop. A packet
arriving in between is punted to the CPU. The counters are zeroed by
the add, so the stats baseline restarts. Should the write-back fail,
the flow is forgotten altogether (its table entries released, its slot
disowned) so that the flowtable's next refresh re-offers it as a new
flow rather than collecting -EEXIST for a zombie. The move is only
noticed when a frame of the moved client reaches the CPU (ARP, a lease
renewal, a new connection): its own hardware-forwarded packets never
reach the bridge, and the switch driver reports no hardware learning.
The work runs under RTNL (DSA tears its ports down under it) and the
driver's own lock, on an ordered workqueue so one move is done before
the next is looked at. A tag_8021q port needs none of this: its bridge
VID leaves the choice to the switch's FDB.

The re-point also reaches a corner of ppe_flow_entry_add() nothing did
before: a counter clear failing after the add itself landed used to
report an error for an entry that was programmed, and every caller
releases the entries it points at on error. It now takes the entry back
out (best effort) before returning the error.

Compile-tested (x86 COMPILE_TEST, W=1, with and without NET_DSA) and
checkpatch clean. Measured on a GL-BE9300 with rtl8_4: a client
re-cabled from lan3 to lan1 during a download was back at 2353 Mbit/s
in hardware at 99.7% idle about 7 s later, 5 s of which were the link
coming up, with "moved to lan1: 2 flow(s) re-pointed, 0 not re-pointed"
in the log. On tag_8021q, where the switch's FDB does the following, a
move the other way (lan1 to lan3, on an earlier build of the same path)
resumed at 2354 Mbit/s with no driver involvement.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/ppe_offload.c
@@ -58,6 +58,9 @@
  *   bridge, a port behind a LAG conduit, a VLAN push on top of a DSA
  *   port, a tagger this code has no encapsulation for, and a conduit
  *   whose parser is not programmed for that tagger are all declined.
+ *   Where the inserts name the port outright (rtl8_4), a client that
+ *   its own VLAN-unaware bridge later learns on another of its ports
+ *   has its flows re-pointed there, see ppe_offload_fdb_event().
  * - Tagged ingress (the reply direction on a tagged WAN) arrives as a
  *   FLOW_DISSECTOR_KEY_VLAN match on the real device plus a
  *   FLOW_ACTION_VLAN_POP. The flow key cannot carry the VLAN, only the
@@ -135,10 +138,12 @@
  * (measured, both taggers).
  */
 
+#include <linux/atomic.h>
 #include <linux/bitfield.h>
 #include <linux/dsa/8021q.h>
 #include <linux/errno.h>
 #include <linux/etherdevice.h>
+#include <linux/hashtable.h>
 #include <linux/if_bridge.h>
 #include <linux/if_ether.h>
 #include <linux/if_vlan.h>
@@ -152,9 +157,12 @@
 #include <net/dsa.h>
 #include <linux/rhashtable.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
+#include <linux/spinlock.h>
 #include <linux/tcp.h>
+#include <linux/workqueue.h>
 #include <net/flow_offload.h>
 #include <net/pkt_cls.h>
+#include <net/switchdev.h>
 
 #include "edma_port.h"
 #include "ppe.h"
@@ -199,6 +207,27 @@ struct ppe_offload_flow_entry {
 	bool flow_valid;
 	/* Only an SNAT flow holds a public IP table entry. */
 	bool pub_ip_valid;
+	/* Set when the nexthop's tag inserts name a DSA user port outright
+	 * (struct ppe_offload_egress::dsa_fixed) and that port is in a
+	 * VLAN-unaware bridge: the entry sits in
+	 * ppe_offload_priv::dsa_fixed_ht so that the bridge learning @dmac
+	 * on another of its ports on the same switch can re-point it, for
+	 * which the programmed nexthop and flow entry are kept. @dsa_ds is
+	 * only ever compared, never dereferenced.
+	 */
+	bool dsa_fixed;
+	/* A move for @dmac is queued and has not looked at this entry yet;
+	 * under dsa_fixed_lock.
+	 */
+	bool move_pending;
+	struct hlist_node dsa_fixed_hnode;
+	const struct dsa_switch *dsa_ds;
+	int dsa_ifindex;
+	int dsa_bridge_ifindex;
+	unsigned int dsa_index;
+	u8 dmac[ETH_ALEN];
+	struct ppe_nexthop_entry nexthop;
+	struct ppe_flow_ipv4_nat_entry flow_ipv4;
 	/* Hardware counters are cumulative and not clear-on-read, so keep the
 	 * previous reading and report the difference. lastused is refreshed only
 	 * when the packet count actually moves, which is what tells the flowtable
@@ -240,6 +269,17 @@ static const struct rhashtable_params pp
  * @flow_table, allocating from the pools, and programming hardware --
  * against concurrent calls for different flows, mirroring
  * mtk_ppe_offload.c's single mtk_flow_offload_mutex.
+ * @dsa_fixed_ht: Flows whose tag inserts name a DSA user port outright,
+ * hashed by destination MAC, see ppe_offload_fdb_event(). Modified under
+ * @lock and @dsa_fixed_lock together, read under either; an entry's port
+ * (dsa_ifindex) is updated under @lock with WRITE_ONCE() for the
+ * notifier's READ_ONCE() under @dsa_fixed_lock.
+ * @dsa_fixed_lock: Lets the atomic switchdev notifier look @dsa_fixed_ht up.
+ * @fdb_wq: Ordered workqueue the FDB-move follower runs on, so that a
+ * move's re-pointing is done by the time a later move is looked at.
+ * @fdb_nb: The switchdev notifier that feeds it.
+ * @fdb_moves: Move work items queued and not yet done, see
+ * PPE_OFFLOAD_FDB_MOVES_MAX.
  */
 struct ppe_offload_priv {
 	struct ppe_device *ppe_dev;
@@ -250,6 +290,11 @@ struct ppe_offload_priv {
 	struct ppe_offload_flow_entry **flow_owner;
 	bool tcp_exceptions_ok;
 	struct mutex lock;
+	DECLARE_HASHTABLE(dsa_fixed_ht, 9);
+	spinlock_t dsa_fixed_lock; /* see @dsa_fixed_ht */
+	struct workqueue_struct *fdb_wq;
+	struct notifier_block fdb_nb;
+	atomic_t fdb_moves;
 };
 
 static LIST_HEAD(ppe_offload_block_cb_list);
@@ -387,6 +432,9 @@ static bool ppe_offload_rule_has_vlan_ke
  * @ctag: Insert a C-VLAN tag with @cvid on egress.
  * @cvid: TCI of that tag.
  * @dsa_index: Switch port index when the egress is a DSA user port.
+ * @bridge_ifindex: ifindex of the VLAN-unaware bridge that port is in, or 0.
+ * @dsa_ifindex: That user port's ifindex.
+ * @dsa_ds: That user port's switch, for identity comparison only.
  * @dsa: Whether the egress is a DSA user port at all.
  * @dsa_fixed: Whether the tag inserts name that port outright (rather than
  * leaving the choice to the switch's own FDB), see ppe_offload_dsa_egress().
@@ -398,6 +446,9 @@ struct ppe_offload_egress {
 	bool ctag;
 	u16 cvid;
 	unsigned int dsa_index;
+	int dsa_ifindex;
+	const struct dsa_switch *dsa_ds;
+	int bridge_ifindex;
 	bool dsa;
 	bool dsa_fixed;
 };
@@ -519,6 +570,7 @@ static int ppe_offload_dsa_egress(struct
 		bridge = NULL;
 	if (bridge) {
 		vlan_aware = br_vlan_enabled(bridge);
+		egress->bridge_ifindex = bridge->ifindex;
 		/* Read once: this is the DSA bookkeeping the residual race
 		 * in the function comment is about.
 		 */
@@ -571,6 +623,8 @@ static int ppe_offload_dsa_egress(struct
 	egress->dev = conduit;
 	egress->dsa = true;
 	egress->dsa_index = dp->index;
+	egress->dsa_ifindex = odev->ifindex;
+	egress->dsa_ds = dp->ds;
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -1199,6 +1253,22 @@ static int ppe_offload_flow_replace(stru
 	entry->lastused = jiffies;
 	priv->flow_owner[flow_index] = entry;
 
+	/* An unbridged port has no FDB to move the client with. */
+	if (egress.dsa_fixed && egress.bridge_ifindex) {
+		entry->dsa_fixed = true;
+		entry->dsa_ds = egress.dsa_ds;
+		entry->dsa_ifindex = egress.dsa_ifindex;
+		entry->dsa_bridge_ifindex = egress.bridge_ifindex;
+		entry->dsa_index = egress.dsa_index;
+		ether_addr_copy(entry->dmac, eth.bytes);
+		entry->nexthop = nexthop;
+		entry->flow_ipv4 = flow_ipv4;
+		spin_lock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+		hash_add(priv->dsa_fixed_ht, &entry->dsa_fixed_hnode,
+			 ether_addr_to_u64(entry->dmac));
+		spin_unlock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	}
+
 	mutex_unlock(&priv->lock);
 
 	pr_debug("ppe_offload: accepted %s flow_index=%u host_index=%u nexthop_index=%u src=%pI4->%pI4 dst=%pI4->%pI4 odev=%s egress=%s\n",
@@ -1262,6 +1332,11 @@ static int ppe_offload_flow_destroy(stru
 	}
 
 	rhashtable_remove_fast(&priv->flow_table, &entry->node, ppe_offload_flow_ht_params);
+	if (entry->dsa_fixed) {
+		spin_lock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+		hash_del(&entry->dsa_fixed_hnode);
+		spin_unlock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	}
 
 	/* Clear flow_owner[] by pointer identity, unconditionally on
 	 * flow_valid: if this entry's own add later failed after already
@@ -1293,6 +1368,359 @@ static int ppe_offload_flow_destroy(stru
 	return 0;
 }
 
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_DSA) && IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_NET_SWITCHDEV)
+/* Following bridge FDB moves for flows that name a switch port outright.
+ *
+ * A flow towards an rtl8_4 user port carries the port in its emulated head
+ * tag (see ppe_offload_dsa_egress()), so hardware keeps delivering to the
+ * port the flow was resolved for even after the client has moved to another
+ * one. The software flowtable has the same blind spot -- its direct transmit
+ * path is bound to the port it resolved at creation -- and nothing lets a
+ * driver tear a flow down (the cookie is opaque). What the driver can do is
+ * follow the move: the bridge learns the client's new port from the first
+ * frame that reaches the CPU and reports it as SWITCHDEV_FDB_ADD_TO_DEVICE
+ * on the new port device, and every fixed-port flow for that MAC is then
+ * re-pointed at the new port, in place. That first frame is the limit of
+ * this: a moved client's own hardware-forwarded packets never reach the
+ * bridge, so the move is noticed when the client next ARPs, renews its
+ * lease or opens a connection (the switch driver reports no hardware
+ * learning to the bridge), and until then its inbound traffic stays on the
+ * old port, exactly as the software flowtable would leave it for good. A
+ * tag_8021q port needs none of this: its bridge VID leaves the choice to
+ * the switch's own FDB.
+ *
+ * Only a move within the flow's own VLAN-unaware bridge counts, learned
+ * without a VLAN: that is the one domain in which the bridge itself would
+ * send the client's traffic to the new port. Another bridge on the same
+ * switch learning the address (a guest port claiming a trusted client's
+ * MAC) must not pull the flow across, which it otherwise would, since the
+ * emulated head tag addresses the port outright with no FDB or VLAN check
+ * in the switch. Within the bridge the follower trusts learning exactly as
+ * far as the bridge does: a port spoofing a client's MAC diverts the
+ * client's new traffic in software and its established hardware flows
+ * here.
+ *
+ * The notifier chain is atomic, so the work is done from a workqueue, and
+ * only queued when the address has a fixed-port flow in that bridge that
+ * is not already waiting for a move (the bridge notifies every address it
+ * learns, at the rate a host cares to make up source MACs, and a host
+ * with two ports can flap one address between them as fast as it likes);
+ * a small cap on the items in flight bounds the rest. The queued item pins
+ * the port device.
+ *
+ * Measured on a GL-BE9300: a client re-cabled from lan3 to lan1 during a
+ * download was back at 2353 Mbit/s in hardware about 7 s later, 5 s of
+ * which were the link coming up, with both of its flows re-pointed.
+ */
+/* Move work items in flight at most; a learn arriving beyond that is
+ * dropped, and the next learn of the address re-arms it.
+ */
+#define PPE_OFFLOAD_FDB_MOVES_MAX	64
+
+struct ppe_offload_fdb_move {
+	struct work_struct work;
+	struct ppe_offload_priv *priv;
+	struct net_device *dev;
+	netdevice_tracker tracker;
+	u8 addr[ETH_ALEN];
+};
+
+/* Drop a tracked flow whose hardware entry is already gone: release its
+ * table entries and forget its cookie, so that a later refresh from the
+ * flowtable re-offers it as a new flow. Called under priv->lock.
+ */
+static void ppe_offload_flow_forget(struct ppe_offload_priv *priv,
+				    struct ppe_offload_flow_entry *entry)
+{
+	rhashtable_remove_fast(&priv->flow_table, &entry->node, ppe_offload_flow_ht_params);
+	if (entry->dsa_fixed) {
+		spin_lock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+		hash_del(&entry->dsa_fixed_hnode);
+		spin_unlock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	}
+	if (priv->flow_owner[entry->flow_index] == entry)
+		priv->flow_owner[entry->flow_index] = NULL;
+	ppe_host_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->host_pool, entry->host_index);
+	ppe_nexthop_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->nexthop_pool, entry->nexthop_index);
+	if (entry->pub_ip_valid)
+		ppe_pub_ip_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->pub_ip_pool, entry->pub_ip_index);
+	kfree(entry);
+}
+
+/* Re-point one fixed-port flow at @dp. Called under priv->lock.
+ *
+ * The key of the flow entry is unchanged, so it belongs in the slot
+ * hardware hashed it to: the entry is deleted and written back at that
+ * same index (PPE_FLOW_OP_MODE_INDEX) with the new nexthop; an
+ * index-mode write to the hashed slot is found by lookups like the
+ * hash-mode add was (measured: re-pointed flows resumed in hardware). A
+ * packet arriving in between is punted to the CPU, nothing more. The
+ * counters are zeroed by the add, so the stats baseline restarts with
+ * them.
+ *
+ * Return: 0 on success, a negative error code on failure. If the
+ * hardware entry is gone by then, the tracked flow is dropped altogether
+ * (ppe_offload_flow_forget()) so that the flowtable's next refresh
+ * re-offers it as a new flow instead of getting -EEXIST for a zombie.
+ */
+static int ppe_offload_flow_repoint(struct ppe_offload_priv *priv,
+				    struct ppe_offload_flow_entry *entry,
+				    const struct dsa_port *dp, const struct net_device *dev)
+{
+	struct ppe_flow_ipv4_nat_entry flow_ipv4 = entry->flow_ipv4;
+	struct ppe_nexthop_entry nexthop = entry->nexthop;
+	u32 flow_words[PPE_FLOW_TBL_ENTRY_WORDS];
+	u32 flow_index = entry->flow_index;
+	u32 nexthop_index;
+	int ret;
+
+	nexthop.cvid = BIT(dp->index);
+	ret = ppe_nexthop_pool_get_and_ref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->nexthop_pool,
+					   &nexthop, &nexthop_index);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	flow_ipv4.nat_nexthop = nexthop_index;
+	ppe_flow_ipv4_nat_entry_encode(&flow_ipv4, flow_words);
+
+	ret = ppe_flow_entry_del(priv->ppe_dev, entry->flow_index);
+	if (ret)
+		goto deref_new;
+
+	ret = ppe_flow_entry_add(priv->ppe_dev, PPE_FLOW_OP_MODE_INDEX, flow_words, &flow_index);
+	if (ret) {
+		/* The old entry is gone. Disown the slot so nothing deletes
+		 * whatever lands there next, and forget the flow.
+		 */
+		if (priv->flow_owner[entry->flow_index] == entry)
+			priv->flow_owner[entry->flow_index] = NULL;
+		entry->flow_valid = false;
+		ppe_nexthop_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->nexthop_pool, nexthop_index);
+		ppe_offload_flow_forget(priv, entry);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	ppe_nexthop_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->nexthop_pool, entry->nexthop_index);
+	entry->nexthop_index = nexthop_index;
+	entry->nexthop = nexthop;
+	entry->flow_ipv4 = flow_ipv4;
+	/* The notifier reads this under dsa_fixed_lock only. */
+	WRITE_ONCE(entry->dsa_ifindex, dev->ifindex);
+	entry->dsa_index = dp->index;
+	entry->stats_packets = 0;
+	entry->stats_bytes = 0;
+
+	return 0;
+
+deref_new:
+	ppe_nexthop_pool_deref(priv->ppe_dev, &priv->nexthop_pool, nexthop_index);
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static void ppe_offload_fdb_move_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct ppe_offload_fdb_move *move = container_of(work, struct ppe_offload_fdb_move, work);
+	struct ppe_offload_priv *priv = move->priv;
+	struct ppe_offload_flow_entry *entry;
+	unsigned int moved = 0, failed = 0;
+	struct edma_port_priv *port_priv;
+	struct net_device *bridge;
+	struct net_device *conduit;
+	struct hlist_node *tmp;
+	struct dsa_port *dp;
+
+	/* DSA tears its ports down under RTNL, and the device reference
+	 * alone keeps only the netdevice, not its port, alive.
+	 */
+	rtnl_lock();
+
+	/* The port may have left DSA, or its bridge, while this was queued. */
+	dp = dsa_port_from_netdev(move->dev);
+	if (IS_ERR(dp) || dp->index >= PPE_OFFLOAD_RTL8_4_RX_BITS)
+		goto out;
+	bridge = dsa_port_bridge_dev_get(dp);
+	if (!bridge || br_vlan_enabled(bridge))
+		goto out;
+
+	/* The bridge learns on a port before it forwards to it; a port it
+	 * would not deliver to is no destination for hardware either.
+	 */
+	if (dp->stp_state != BR_STATE_FORWARDING)
+		goto out;
+
+	/* What ppe_offload_dsa_egress() required of the original port: the
+	 * new one must hang off the same aliased EDMA conduit, whose PPE
+	 * port the kept nexthop names.
+	 */
+	if (dp->cpu_port_in_lag)
+		goto out;
+	conduit = dp->cpu_dp->conduit;
+	if (!conduit || !edma_port_is_edma_netdev(conduit))
+		goto out;
+	port_priv = netdev_priv(conduit);
+	if (READ_ONCE(port_priv->parser_state) != EDMA_PARSER_DSA_RTL8_4)
+		goto out;
+
+	mutex_lock(&priv->lock);
+
+	/* @lock keeps the entries alive; a failed re-point drops its entry. */
+	hash_for_each_possible_safe(priv->dsa_fixed_ht, entry, tmp, dsa_fixed_hnode,
+				    ether_addr_to_u64(move->addr)) {
+		if (!entry->flow_valid || entry->dsa_ds != dp->ds ||
+		    entry->nexthop.port != port_priv->ppe_port->port_id ||
+		    entry->dsa_bridge_ifindex != bridge->ifindex ||
+		    entry->dsa_ifindex == move->dev->ifindex ||
+		    !ether_addr_equal(entry->dmac, move->addr))
+			continue;
+
+		if (ppe_offload_flow_repoint(priv, entry, dp, move->dev))
+			failed++;
+		else
+			moved++;
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&priv->lock);
+
+	/* A MAC flapping between two ports (a loop, a misbehaving client)
+	 * would otherwise turn this into a log storm.
+	 */
+	if (moved || failed)
+		pr_info_ratelimited("ppe_offload: %pM moved to %s: %u flow(s) re-pointed, %u not re-pointed\n",
+				    move->addr, netdev_name(move->dev), moved, failed);
+
+out:
+	rtnl_unlock();
+	/* Whether or not the move was applied, let the next learn of this
+	 * address queue again.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	hash_for_each_possible(priv->dsa_fixed_ht, entry, dsa_fixed_hnode,
+			       ether_addr_to_u64(move->addr)) {
+		if (ether_addr_equal(entry->dmac, move->addr))
+			entry->move_pending = false;
+	}
+	spin_unlock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	atomic_dec(&priv->fdb_moves);
+	netdev_put(move->dev, &move->tracker);
+	kfree(move);
+}
+
+static int ppe_offload_fdb_event(struct notifier_block *nb, unsigned long event, void *ptr)
+{
+	struct ppe_offload_priv *priv = container_of(nb, struct ppe_offload_priv, fdb_nb);
+	struct net_device *dev = switchdev_notifier_info_to_dev(ptr);
+	const struct switchdev_notifier_fdb_info *fdb_info = ptr;
+	struct ppe_offload_flow_entry *entry;
+	struct ppe_offload_fdb_move *move;
+	struct net_device *bridge;
+	bool wanted = false;
+
+	if (event != SWITCHDEV_FDB_ADD_TO_DEVICE)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	/* The bridge reports a local (its own) address against the bridge
+	 * device, only a DSA user port can be the new home of a client this
+	 * driver addresses by port, a VLAN-aware bridge's entries carry a
+	 * VID this cannot follow, and a locked entry (MAB) is the bridge
+	 * withholding forwarding, not granting it.
+	 */
+	if (fdb_info->is_local || fdb_info->vid || fdb_info->locked ||
+	    !dsa_user_dev_check(dev))
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	/* The chain runs under RCU, which is what the upper lookup needs. */
+	bridge = netdev_master_upper_dev_get_rcu(dev);
+	if (!bridge || !netif_is_bridge_master(bridge))
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	if (atomic_read(&priv->fdb_moves) >= PPE_OFFLOAD_FDB_MOVES_MAX)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	/* Only an address with a live fixed-port flow of this very bridge
+	 * on some other port, not already waiting for a move, is worth a
+	 * work item; everything is re-checked under RTNL there.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	hash_for_each_possible(priv->dsa_fixed_ht, entry, dsa_fixed_hnode,
+			       ether_addr_to_u64(fdb_info->addr)) {
+		if (!ether_addr_equal(entry->dmac, fdb_info->addr) ||
+		    entry->dsa_bridge_ifindex != bridge->ifindex ||
+		    READ_ONCE(entry->dsa_ifindex) == dev->ifindex ||
+		    entry->move_pending)
+			continue;
+		entry->move_pending = true;
+		wanted = true;
+	}
+	spin_unlock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+
+	if (!wanted)
+		return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+	move = kzalloc(sizeof(*move), GFP_ATOMIC);
+	if (!move)
+		goto unmark;
+	atomic_inc(&priv->fdb_moves);
+
+	INIT_WORK(&move->work, ppe_offload_fdb_move_work);
+	move->priv = priv;
+	netdev_hold(dev, &move->tracker, GFP_ATOMIC);
+	move->dev = dev;
+	ether_addr_copy(move->addr, fdb_info->addr);
+	queue_work(priv->fdb_wq, &move->work);
+
+	return NOTIFY_DONE;
+
+unmark:
+	spin_lock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+	hash_for_each_possible(priv->dsa_fixed_ht, entry, dsa_fixed_hnode,
+			       ether_addr_to_u64(fdb_info->addr)) {
+		if (ether_addr_equal(entry->dmac, fdb_info->addr))
+			entry->move_pending = false;
+	}
+	spin_unlock_bh(&priv->dsa_fixed_lock);
+
+	return NOTIFY_DONE;
+}
